    "body": "### Problem\n\nFor a buffer named with a URL, such as `fugitive:///repo/.git//0/models/a.sql`, `vim.filetype.match()` passes `vim.fs.abspath(name)` to pattern and function matchers. `abspath` treats the URL as a relative path, so a filetype function gets `<cwd>/fugitive:/repo/.git/0/models/a.sql`, and one that walks up from the path, to find a project file say, has to strip the cwd and scheme back off. `match()` already skips URL names in its directory check (#40695), and master still calls `vim.fs.abspath(name)` here.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```lua\nlocal seen\nvim.filetype.add({ pattern = { [\".*%.sql\"] = function(path) seen = path end } })\nvim.filetype.match({ filename = \"fugitive:///repo/.git//0/models/a.sql\" })\nprint(seen)\n```\n\n`nvim --clean -l repro.lua`, run from `/tmp`, prints `/tmp/fugitive:/repo/.git/0/models/a.sql`.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe function receives the name as the buffer has it, `fugitive:///repo/.git//0/models/a.sql`.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.12.6, Build type: Release, LuaJIT 2.1.1792588164\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNo, `vim.filetype` is Nvim only.\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nUbuntu 24.04.5 LTS (WSL2)\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nWezTerm (not terminal-specific)\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\ntmux-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nHomebrew\n",
